# Target C-II — coupled physical-contact floor from the exact tooth/long-side burden

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Repository base:** `6f76ec45a65aa0475f31c940c490c3c5b60694fb`  
**Status:** proved proof-only composition; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** no-container/full-core Type-II low-`t` branch  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The exact outer-boundary theorem gives a coupled burden

\[
N_2+2L_{>2}\ge x_B-2d_{out},
\]

where `N_2` counts genuine two-edge teeth and `L_{>2}` is the loaded-hull side
length above two. Previous notes first split this burden into a tooth branch or a
long-side branch and only then physicalized the chosen branch. That loses information.

Both physicalizations are linear. Optimizing them simultaneously against the exact
coupled burden gives a stronger branch-independent conclusion. At the present low-`t`
endpoint every survivor carries at least

\[
\boxed{0.2025300201\,D-o(D)}
\]

distinct robustly non-tangential unit contacts in one of the two explicit boundary
families.

The proof also sharpens the convenient shallow-tooth threshold from `4/15` to
`15/56`. The choice is almost extremal and has an exact one-unit square margin:

\[
\left({97\over112}\right)^2-{3\over4}={1\over12544}>0.
\]

## 1. Tooth physicalization with `eta=15/56`

Put

\[
\eta={15\over56},
\qquad
\alpha_\eta=\arccos(1-\eta/2).
\]

Then

\[
\cos\alpha_\eta={97\over112}>{\sqrt3\over2},
\]

so `alpha_eta<pi/6`. The global-turn shallow-tooth theorem therefore applies: all but
at most two `eta`-shallow teeth have distinct unloaded apices.

The exact tooth-detour deletion gives

\[
N_{sh}(\eta)\ge N_2-{d_{out}\over\eta}.
\]

Choosing one inward contact at every distinct shallow apex and dividing only by the
two endpoints of a physical edge gives the finite estimate

\[
\boxed{
E_T
\ge {1\over2}
\left(N_2-{d_{out}\over\eta}-2\right).
}
\tag{CP.1}
\]

Every selected contact has inward normal component greater than

\[
Y_\eta
=\sin(\pi/3-\alpha_\eta)
={97\sqrt3-\sqrt{3135}\over224}
=0.5000797215067117\ldots>{1\over2}.
\tag{CP.2}
\]

Thus the threshold is very close to the limiting value `2-sqrt(3)` while preserving
a strict source-level directional margin.

## 2. Long-side physicalization

Set

\[
\beta={\pi\over6},
\qquad
c_\beta:=1-\cos\beta=1-{\sqrt3\over2}.
\]

The directional-energy theorem gives at least

\[
S_\beta
\ge L_{>2}-{2d_{out}\over c_\beta}
\]

`beta`-straight internal outer-route occurrences on long-side intervals.
Occurrence-injective transverse selection and bridge suppression give

\[
2B_o+2E_L\ge S_\beta,
\]

where `B_o` is the number of outer bridges and every edge counted by `E_L` has
absolute transverse component strictly greater than `1/2` relative to the associated
loaded-hull side direction.

On a full-core Type-II convergent subsequence, write

\[
\delta:=\lim {d_{out}\over D}.
\]

Full-core near-disk area plus bridge-subtracted isoperimetry gives

\[
\limsup {2B_o\over D}\le\delta.
\]

Therefore, if

\[
\ell:=\lim {L_{>2}\over D},
\]

then

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {E_L\over D}
\ge {\ell\over2}
-\delta\left({1\over2}+{1\over c_\beta}\right).
}
\tag{CP.3}
\]

## 3. Optimize both physicalizations before splitting

On the same convergent Type-II subsequence write

\[
n_2:=\lim {N_2\over D},
\qquad
\kappa:=\lim {k\over D}.
\]

The exact outer tooth/long-side burden gives

\[
\boxed{
n_2+2\ell\ge\pi-t-\kappa-\delta.}
\tag{CP.4}
\]

Let

\[
y:=\max\left\{
\liminf {E_T\over D},
\liminf {E_L\over D}
\right\}.
\]

Equations (CP.1) and (CP.3) imply

\[
n_2\le2y+{\delta\over\eta},
\]

\[
\ell\le2y+\delta\left(1+{2\over c_\beta}\right).
\]

Insert these upper bounds into (CP.4). One obtains

\[
\boxed{
6y
\ge
\pi-t-\kappa-K\delta,
}
\tag{CP.5}
\]

with

\[
\begin{aligned}
K
&=3+{1\over\eta}+{4\over c_\beta}\\
&=3+{56\over15}+8(2+\sqrt3)\\
&={341\over15}+8\sqrt3\\
&=36.58973979388435\ldots .
\end{aligned}
\tag{CP.6}
\]

This is the gain that is lost if one replaces (CP.4) by the crude
`max{N_2,L_{>2}}` bound first.

## 4. Use the low-`t` network squeeze jointly, not independently

Proof-only AG42 plus audited Section 171 gives

\[
2\delta+{\kappa\over2}\le R(t),
\qquad
R(t):={9t\over2}-2\pi-C_0,
\]

where

\[
C_0=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.00042,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\]

At fixed `delta`, the right side of (CP.5) is smallest when

\[
\kappa=2R(t)-4\delta.
\]

Since `0<=delta<=R(t)/2` and `K>4`, the remaining expression decreases with
`delta`; hence its worst case is

\[
\delta={R(t)\over2},
\qquad
\kappa=0.
\]

Therefore every low-`t` survivor in this scope obeys

\[
\boxed{
y\ge
{1\over6}
\left[
\pi-t-
\left({341\over30}+4\sqrt3\right)R(t)
\right].}
\tag{CP.7}
\]

This joint optimization is important. Simultaneously substituting the separate worst
bounds `kappa+delta<=2R(t)` and `delta<=R(t)/2` would combine two incompatible
extreme points and lose a real constant.

## 5. Current endpoint

At

\[
t_{42}=C_{146}+0.0013751
=1.8208886379794447\ldots,
\]

one has

\[
R(t_{42})=0.00576795.
\]

Equation (CP.7) gives

\[
\boxed{
y\ge0.2025300201313801518\ldots .}
\tag{CP.8}
\]

Thus at least one of the following holds:

1. there are at least `0.2025300201 D-o(D)` distinct shallow-tooth penetration
   contacts, each with inward component greater than `0.5000797215`; or
2. there are at least `0.2025300201 D-o(D)` distinct long-side transverse contacts,
   each with absolute transverse component greater than `1/2`.

For reference, if the crude tooth-vs-long-side split is taken first, the best current
branch-independent floor is only about `0.195226D`. The coupled optimization recovers
roughly `0.00730D` additional guaranteed physical contacts.

## 6. What changed in the C-II frontier

The low-`t` endpoint can no longer hide behind any of the following linear losses:

- repeated shallow-tooth apices;
- rounded long-side metric mass without physical contacts;
- loaded degree-two repetitions along the outer walk;
- fivefold straight-event multiplicity at one contact vertex; or
- premature splitting of the exact `N_2+2L_{>2}` burden.

The remaining obstruction is genuinely global. A survivor must route at least about
`0.2025D` robust non-tangential boundary contacts through the nearly triangular
pentagon/rhombus train network while simultaneously realizing the near-extremal
Woodall diameter thrackle. Local rhombus-area penalties cannot close this: the
vanishing-shear regression allows the contact directions to approach the triangular
lattice directions with arbitrarily small per-cell excess.

The next useful theorem must therefore control **train occupancy / diameter-brace
reuse** for this forced contact family, or produce a source-preserving boundary
motion. Scalar ledgers alone are already known to be compatible.

## 7. Reproducibility

Run

```bash
python3 research/verify_target_c_coupled_physical_contact_floor.py
```

for the high-precision constant checks.
